Step 1: Verify Docker installation
Open the Ubuntu terminal using Ctrl + Alt + T.
Check Docker:
docker --version
Check whether Docker is running:
  
sudo systemctl status docker --no-pager
Check running containers:
sudo docker ps


If Docker is installed but not running:
sudo systemctl start docker


Meaning: Verifies that Docker is installed and running.
Step 2: Install kubectl
Check whether kubectl is installed:
kubectl version --client


If it is not installed, execute:
sudo snap install kubectl --classic


Verify:
kubectl version --client


Meaning: kubectl is used to communicate with and manage Kubernetes resources.
Step 3: Install Minikube
Check whether Minikube is installed:
minikube version


If it is not installed, open the browser and visit:
Minikube official installation pageDownload the Linux AMD64 binary, assuming the lab computer uses an AMD64 processor.

Open the terminal:
cd ~/Downloads


Install Minikube:
sudo install minikube-linux-amd64 /usr/local/bin/minikube


Verify:
minikube version


Meaning: Minikube creates a local Kubernetes cluster.
Step 4: Start the Kubernetes cluster
Start Minikube using Docker as the driver:
minikube start --driver=docker


Check the status:
minikube status


Check the nodes:
kubectl get nodes


Expected: The node should display Ready.

Important: Minikube normally uses Docker without sudo. If you get a Docker permission error, ask your lab administrator to configure your Docker access. Do not run Minikube with sudo as a workaround.
Step 5: Create the web application folder
Create a folder:
mkdir -p ~/docker-webapp


Navigate into it:
cd ~/docker-webapp


Create the HTML file:
nano index.html


Enter:
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>Docker Web Application</title>
</head>
<body>
    <h1>Hello from Docker!</h1>
    <p>This web application is running inside a Docker container.</p>
    <p>Kubernetes deployment successfully completed.</p>
</body>
</html>


Save using Ctrl + O, press Enter, and exit using Ctrl + X.

Meaning: Creates the web page that will run inside the container.
Step 6: Create the Dockerfile
Execute:
nano Dockerfile


Enter:
FROM nginx:latest
COPY index.html /usr/share/nginx/html/index.html
EXPOSE 80


Save and exit.

Meaning:
- FROM selects the Nginx base image.
- COPY copies the HTML file into the Nginx web directory.
- EXPOSE specifies the application port.
Step 7: Build the Docker image
Make sure you are inside ~/docker-webapp.

Execute:
docker build -t docker-webapp:latest .


Verify:
docker images


Meaning: Builds a Docker image named docker-webapp with the tag latest.
Step 8: Load the image into Minikube
Execute:
minikube image load docker-webapp:latest


Meaning: Loads the locally created Docker image into Minikube so Kubernetes can use it without downloading it from Docker Hub.
Step 9: Create the Kubernetes folder
Navigate to the Home directory:
cd ~


Create the folder:
mkdir -p kubernetes-lab


Navigate into it:
cd ~/kubernetes-lab


The folder structure should look like this:
Home/
│
├── docker-webapp/
│   ├── Dockerfile
│   └── index.html
│
└── kubernetes-lab/
    ├── deployment.yaml
    └── service.yaml


Step 10: Create the Deployment configuration
Create the file:
nano deployment.yaml


Enter the following YAML configuration:

apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: docker-webapp-deployment
spec:
  replicas: 2
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: docker-webapp
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: docker-webapp
    spec:
      containers:
        - name: docker-webapp
          image: docker-webapp:latest
          imagePullPolicy: Never
          ports:
            - containerPort: 80



Save using Ctrl + O, press Enter, and exit using Ctrl + X.

Meaning:
- kind: Deployment creates a Deployment.
- replicas: 2 requests two Pods.
- selector identifies the Pods managed by the Deployment.
- template defines the Pods to be created.
- image specifies the Docker image.
- containerPort: 80 specifies the application port.
Step 11: Deploy the application
Execute:
kubectl apply -f deployment.yaml


Meaning: Creates the Deployment using the YAML configuration file.
Step 12: Verify the Deployment
kubectl get deployments


Expected: The Deployment should eventually show 2/2 ready replicas.
Step 13: Verify the Pods
kubectl get pods


Expected: Two Pods should appear with Running status and 1/1 readiness.
Step 14: Create the Service configuration
Create the file:
nano service.yaml


Enter:

apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: docker-webapp-service
spec:
  type: NodePort
  selector:
    app: docker-webapp
  ports:
    - protocol: TCP
      port: 80
      targetPort: 80



Save and exit.

Meaning:
- kind: Service creates a Service.
- type: NodePort exposes the application through a node port.
- selector connects the Service to the application Pods.
- port specifies the Service port.
- targetPort specifies the container port.
Step 15: Apply the Service configuration
Execute:
kubectl apply -f service.yaml


Verify:
kubectl get services


Meaning: Creates and verifies the Service.
Step 16: Access the web application
Execute:
minikube service docker-webapp-service


The browser will open the application URL, or Minikube will display the URL in the terminal.
Hello from Docker!
This web application is running inside a Docker container.

Kubernetes deployment successfully completed.
Application deployed

Step 17: Verify the application inside a Pod
List the Pods:
kubectl get pods


Copy one Pod name and execute:
kubectl exec -it <pod-name> -- cat /usr/share/nginx/html/index.html


Replace <pod-name> with the actual Pod name.

Meaning: Displays the HTML file stored inside the running container.
Step 18: Test scaling
Increase the number of replicas to three:
kubectl scale deployment docker-webapp-deployment --replicas=3


Verify:
kubectl get pods


Three Pods should appear once scaling completes.

Restore the original number of replicas:
kubectl scale deployment docker-webapp-deployment --replicas=2


Verify again:
kubectl get pods


Meaning: Demonstrates Kubernetes scaling by changing the number of application replicas.
Step 19: Clean up after the experiment (optional)
Delete the Service:
kubectl delete service docker-webapp-service


Delete the Deployment:
kubectl delete deployment docker-webapp-deployment


Stop Minikube:
minikube stop


Meaning: Removes the experiment's Kubernetes resources and stops the local cluster. Do this only after completing the experiment and capturing the required outputs.
4. Result
The containerized web application was successfully deployed on a Kubernetes cluster using Minikube. A Deployment was created to manage two Pod replicas, a NodePort Service was configured to expose the application, and scaling was tested by increasing and decreasing the number of Pods.
